Yes. And “translation engine” is stronger than a metaphor if we use it carefully.

Life continually converts one kind of constraint or signal into another: photons into electrochemical activity, molecules into taste and smell, mechanical pressure into touch, DNA sequences into proteins, nutrients into gradients and motion, hormones into altered behavior, experience into neural structure, behavior into environmental change, and environmental change back into selection pressure.

So the deeper pattern is something like:

world → detection → representation → transformation → action → changed world → new detection

But life does more than translate. It also filters, compresses, remembers, predicts, repairs, reproduces, and invents. An organism does not merely receive reality and translate it faithfully. It decides—through evolved structure—what distinctions matter. A tick, an oak tree, an octopus, and a human can inhabit the same physical world while effectively living inside very different informational worlds.

And then there is a second layer: living things change the very language they are translating. Bones remodel under load. Immune systems learn. Brains rewire. Beavers create ponds. Humans create words, roads, laws, computers, music. The output becomes part of the next input.

That produces a richer loop:

reality → embodiment → meaning → action → structure → altered reality

Which connects directly to what you were saying about materials and behavior being encoded structurally. Eventually a successful translation can become frozen into architecture. What was once an active solution becomes morphology, instinct, convention, infrastructure, or code.

You could almost define evolution as translation with inheritance.

And intelligence as translation that can begin examining and deliberately rewriting its own translators.